1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:08,428
This is Apsis. It is an application for the
design of flights through the solar system.

2
00:00:08,468 --> 00:00:13,492
Apsis operates in your web browser. It
does not use a server or an account.

3
00:00:13,892 --> 00:00:18,399
This is the solar system on 14 October 2026.

4
00:00:18,439 --> 00:00:28,600
The positions of the planets come from DE440, an
ephemeris of JPL. The data covers the years 1950 to 2099.

5
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:32,392
Apsis has two modes: Explorer and Expert.

6
00:00:32,432 --> 00:00:38,845
In the Explorer mode, you select a destination.
Apsis finds a launch date and calculates the flight.

7
00:00:38,885 --> 00:00:43,349
In the Expert mode, you set all the
values, and you see all the numbers.

8
00:00:43,949 --> 00:00:47,648
For example, select Mars.
Then click the launch button.

9
00:00:47,688 --> 00:00:51,403
Apsis examines the launch
dates of the next 27 months.

10
00:00:51,443 --> 00:00:56,575
For each launch date, it finds the transfer
with the smallest change of velocity.

11
00:00:56,775 --> 00:01:06,238
This chart shows two launch windows. Their values are
almost equal: 3.634 and 3.626 kilometers per second.

12
00:01:06,278 --> 00:01:11,730
Apsis selects the lower value:
a launch on 29 November 2028.

13
00:01:11,931 --> 00:01:17,934
Then it calculates the flight. The
spacecraft gets to Mars after 315 days.

14
00:01:18,834 --> 00:01:24,210
No path in Apsis is a drawing. Each
path is the result of a calculation.

15
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:27,927
First, a simple model uses
only the gravity of the Sun.

16
00:01:27,967 --> 00:01:34,649
Then a numerical integration adds the gravity of the
planets, the Moon, Pluto and three large asteroids.

17
00:01:34,689 --> 00:01:43,172
Then Apsis adjusts the velocity at the start. Thus the
spacecraft goes near Mars at an altitude of 500 kilometers.

18
00:01:43,772 --> 00:01:49,432
Apsis also contains 8 actual missions,
from Pioneer 11 to Mars 2020.

19
00:01:49,472 --> 00:01:55,626
It calculates these flights again and compares
the results with data from JPL Horizons.

20
00:01:55,666 --> 00:02:04,189
For Voyager 2, the distances of closest approach at
Jupiter, Saturn and Uranus agree within 1.02 percent.

21
00:02:05,090 --> 00:02:09,472
The next three videos tell you
about orbits, transfers and flybys.
